  "slug": "recovery-model-2949",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply quantitative models to infer physiological states and parameters from meal, redox, and bile acid data.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a quantitative modeling approach that links measurable outputs to mechanistic parameters or state transitions. A mouse meal-level food intake model, validated on five studies, is extended across diets and GLP-1 receptor agonist treatments and used to simulate populations. An eleven-variable network model of Nrf1 and Nrf2 coupling, built from HepG2 and murine hepatocyte multi-omics, identifies a bistable attractor under persistent stress, indicating multiple stable redox states under sustained stress. A surrogate-accelerated Bayesian pipeline combining timescale model reduction and neural surrogates infers enterohepatic bile acid parameters, with temporal blood sampling improving parameter recovery. The shared element is pairing reduced or surrogate-based computation with multi-scale data to make parameter inference and dynamical analysis tractable.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
